{"id":"neet-2026r-q039","pn":39,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Thermal expansion of volume","q":"The temperature of a metallic sphere of radius R is increased by a small amount ΔT. If the linear coefficient of thermal expansion of the metal is α, the approximate increase in the volume of the sphere is:","o":["3πR³αΔT","4πR³αΔT","6πR³αΔT","2πR³αΔT"],"c":1,"s":"For the metallic sphere, ΔR/R = αΔT.\nVolume of the sphere V = (4)/(3)πR³.\nSo ΔV/V = 3ΔR/R.\nIncrease in volume ΔV = 3(ΔR/R)((4)/(3)πR³) = 4πR³αΔT."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q019","pn":19,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Heat conduction in series","q":"Three identical heat conducting rods are connected in series as shown in the figure. The rods on the sides have thermal conductivity 2K while that in the middle has thermal conductivity K. The left end of the combination is maintained at temperature 3T and the right end at T. The rods are thermally insulated from outside. In steady state, temperature at the left junction is T₁ and that at the right junction is T₂. The ratio T₁/T₂ is","o":["3/2","4/3","5/3","5/4"],"c":2,"s":"In series, R_eq = R₁ + R₂ + R₃ = l/(2KA) + l/(KA) + l/(2KA) = 4l/(2KA)\nR_eq = 2l/(KA)\nIn series the rate of heat flow is the same.\n(3T − T₁)/R₁ = (3T − T)/R_eq\n(3T − T₁)(2KA)/l = (2T)(KA)/(2l)\n6T − 2T₁ = T\n2T₁ = 5T\nT₁ = 5T/2 ...(1)\nNow equate the heat flow in the 3rd section and the total.\n(T₂ − T)/R₃ = (3T − T)/R_eq\n(T₂ − T)(2KA)/l = 2T(KA)/(2l)\n2T₂ − 2T = T\nT₂ = 3T/2 ...(2)\nFrom (1) and (2): T₁/T₂ = (5T × 2)/(2 × 3T) = 5/3","fig":{"w":310,"h":100,"alt":"Three rods in series: 2K, K and 2K, left end wall at 3T and right end wall at T; the junctions are T1 (left) and T2 (right).","d":[["r",49.4,20.9,204.3,46.6,""],["l",115.9,20.9,115.9,67.5,""],["l",184.3,20.9,184.3,67.5,""],["l",48.4,5.7,48.4,88.4,"B"],["l",253.7,5.7,253.7,88.4,"B"],["l",36.1,20.9,48.4,12.4,""],["l",253.7,20.9,266,12.4,""],["l",36.1,28.5,48.4,20,""],["l",253.7,28.5,266,20,""],["l",36.1,36.1,48.4,27.6,""],["l",253.7,36.1,266,27.6,""],["l",36.1,43.7,48.4,35.2,""],["l",253.7,43.7,266,35.2,""],["l",36.1,51.3,48.4,42.8,""],["l",253.7,51.3,266,42.8,""],["l",36.1,58.9,48.4,50.3,""],["l",253.7,58.9,266,50.3,""],["l",36.1,66.5,48.4,58,""],["l",253.7,66.5,266,58,""],["l",36.1,74.1,48.4,65.6,""],["l",253.7,74.1,266,65.6,""],["l",36.1,81.7,48.4,73.1,""],["l",253.7,81.7,266,73.1,""],["l",36.1,89.3,48.4,80.8,""],["l",253.7,89.3,266,80.8,""],["t",11.4,49.4,"3T",""],["t",77,49.4,"2K","m"],["t",150.1,49.4,"K","m"],["t",216.6,49.4,"2K","m"],["t",275.5,49.4,"T",""],["t",116.9,87.4,"T₁","m"],["t",184.3,87.4,"T₂","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q041","pn":41,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Heat conduction in series","q":"Two rods one made of copper and other made of steel of the same length and same cross sectional area are joined together. The thermal conductivity of copper and steel are 385 J s⁻¹ K⁻¹m⁻¹ and 50 J s⁻¹K⁻¹m⁻¹ respectively. The free ends of copper and steel are held at 100°C and 0°C respectively. The temperature at the junction is, nearly:","o":["88.5°C","12°C","50°C","73°C"],"c":0,"s":"In steady state the heat flow in both rods is equal.\nLet the junction temperature be θ. Then K_Cu A(100 − θ)/l = K_steel A(θ − 0)/l.\n385(100 − θ) = 50θ.\n38500 = 435θ, so θ ≈ 88.5°C.","fig":{"w":256,"h":96,"alt":"A copper rod and a steel rod joined end to end: free ends at 100 degrees C and 0 degrees C, junction at theta degrees C, heat flowing left to right as H1 and H2.","d":[["r",8,28,240,24],["l",128,28,128,52],["t",68,20,"Cu","m"],["t",188,20,"Steel","m"],["t",8,72,"100°C"],["l",54,69,100,69,"a"],["t",77,86,"H₁","m"],["t",128,72,"θ°C","m"],["l",144,69,190,69,"a"],["t",167,86,"H₂","m"],["t",248,72,"0°C","e"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2021-q033","pn":33,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Newton's law of cooling","q":"A cup of coffee cools from 90°C to 80°C in t minutes, when the room temperature is 20°C. The time taken by a similar cup of coffee to cool from 80°C to 60°C at a room temperature same at 20°C is","o":["(5)/(13) t","(13)/(10) t","(13)/(5) t","(10)/(13) t"],"c":2,"s":"Use the average form of Newton's law: (T1 – T2)/Δt = K[(T1 + T2)/2 – Ts].\nCase 1: 10/t = K(85 – 20) = 65K, so K = 2/(13t).\nCase 2: 20/t₁ = K(70 – 20) = 50K = 100/(13t).\nt₁ = 20 × 13t/100 = 13t/5."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q132","pn":132,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Heat capacity","q":"The quantities of heat required to raise the temperature of two solid copper spheres of radii r₁ and r₂ (r₁ = 1.5 r₂) through 1 K are in the ratio :","o":["9/4","3/2","5/3","27/8"],"c":3,"s":"Heat Q = msΔT, and s and ΔT are the same.\nSo Q ∝ m = (4/3)πr³ρ ∝ r³.\nQ₁/Q₂ = (r₁/r₂)³ = (1.5)³ = 27/8."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q108","pn":108,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Thermal conductivity","q":"The unit of thermal conductivity is :","o":["W m K⁻¹","W m⁻¹ K⁻¹","J m K^b 1","J m⁻¹ K⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"Heat current H = kAΔθ/ℓ, so k = Hℓ/(AΔθ).\nUnit = W·m/(m²·K) = W m⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nNote: Option 3 is garbled in the source (printed 'J m K^b 1'); written as printed."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q135","pn":135,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Thermal expansion","q":"A copper rod of 88 cm and an aluminium rod of unknown length have their increase in length independent of increase in temperature. The length of aluminium rod is (αCu = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹ and αAl = 2.2 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹)","o":["88 cm","68 cm","6.8 cm","113.9 cm"],"c":1,"s":"The increase in length is the same for both rods at any temperature change.\nΔℓ = ℓαΔT, so ℓ₁α₁ = ℓ₂α₂.\n88 × 1.7×10⁻⁵ = ℓ × 2.2×10⁻⁵.\nℓ = 88 × 1.7/2.2 = 68 cm.\nNote: The printed comment says the question should read 'difference in length' instead of 'increase in length'."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q011","pn":11,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Wien's law and Stefan's law","q":"The power radiated by a black body is P and it radiates maximum energy at wavelength, λ₀. If the temperature of the black body is now changed so that it radiates maximum energy at wavelength (3)/(4)λ₀, the power radiated by it becomes nP. The value of n is","o":["(3)/(4)","(4)/(3)","(256)/(81)","(81)/(256)"],"c":2,"s":"Stefan's law: power ∝ T⁴.\nWien's law: λT = constant, so T ∝ (1)/(λ).\nHence power ∝ (1)/(λ⁴).\n(nP)/(P) = (λ₀/(3λ₀/4))⁴ = ((4)/(3))⁴ = (256)/(81).\nSo n = (256)/(81)."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q169","pn":169,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Thermal conductivity of composite rod","q":"Two rods A and B of different materials are welded together as shown in figure. Their thermal conductivities are K1 and K2. The thermal conductivity of the composite rod will be","o":["2(K1 + K2)","(K1 + K2)/2","3(K1 + K2)/2","K1 + K2"],"c":1,"s":"The rods lie side by side with the same length and area, between the same end temperatures T1 and T2, so heat flows through them in parallel.\nTotal heat flow = (K1 A + K2 A)ΔT/d. The composite has area 2A: K_eq × 2A × ΔT/d.\nEquating: K_eq = (K1 + K2)/2.","fig":{"w":200,"h":125,"alt":"Composite rod of length d: upper half rod A (K1) and lower half rod B (K2) welded along their length, left end at T1 and right end at T2.","d":[["l",40,74,48,82],["l",40,60,52,72],["l",54,74,62,82],["l",40,46,52,58],["l",68,74,76,82],["l",40,32,52,44],["l",54,46,66,58],["l",76,68,90,82],["l",44,22,52,30],["l",68,46,104,82],["l",58,22,66,30],["l",76,40,100,64],["l",110,74,118,82],["l",72,22,108,58],["l",124,74,132,82],["l",86,22,100,36],["l",110,46,122,58],["l",124,60,146,82],["l",100,22,108,30],["l",124,46,160,82],["l",114,22,122,30],["l",124,32,160,68],["l",128,22,160,54],["l",142,22,160,40],["l",156,22,160,26],["r",40,22,120,60],["l",40,52,160,52],["t",64,42,"A","m"],["t",112,42,"K₁","m"],["t",64,70,"B","m"],["t",112,70,"K₂","m"],["t",34,56,"T₁","e"],["t",166,56,"T₂",""],["l",40,98,160,98,"b"],["t",100,114,"d","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2017-q173","pn":173,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Stefan's law","q":"A spherical black body with a radius of 12 cm radiates 450 watt power at 500 K. If the radius were halved and the temperature doubled, the power radiated in watt would be :","o":["1800","225","450","1000"],"c":0,"s":"P = σ(4πR²)T⁴, so P ∝ R²T⁴.\nP2/P1 = (R/2 ÷ R)² × (2T/T)⁴ = (1/4) × 16 = 4.\nP2 = 4 × 450 = 1800 W."},
{"id":"neet-2016b-q015","pn":15,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 24 Jul","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Calorimetry with varying heat capacity","q":"Two identical bodies are made of a material for which the heat capacity increases with temperature. One of these is at 100ºC, while the other one is at 0ºC. If the two bodies are brought into contact, then, assuming no heat loss, the final common temperature is -","o":["50ºC","more than 50ºC","less than 50ºC but greater than 0ºC","0ºC"],"c":1,"s":"Heat gained by the cold body = heat lost by the hot body.\nHeat capacity rises with temperature, so the hot body has the larger heat capacity (c2 > c1).\nc1 (T − 0) = c2 (100 − T), so T/(100 − T) = c2/c1 > 1.\nThen T > 100 − T, so T > 50ºC."},
{"id":"neet-2016b-q016","pn":16,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 24 Jul","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Newton's law of cooling","q":"A body cools from a temperature 3T to 2T in 10 minutes. The room temperature is T. Assume that Newton’s law of cooling is applicable. The temperature of the body at the end of next 10 minutes will be -","o":["(7/4) T","(3/2) T","(4/3) T","T"],"c":1,"s":"Newton's law: fall in temperature per time = k × (mean temperature − room temperature).\nFirst 10 min: (3T − 2T)/10 = k[(3T + 2T)/2 − T] = k(3T/2).\nNext 10 min: (2T − Tf)/10 = k[(2T + Tf)/2 − T] = k(Tf/2).\nDivide the first by the second: T/(2T − Tf) = (3T/2)/(Tf/2) = 3T/Tf.\nSo Tf = 3(2T − Tf) = 6T − 3Tf, giving 4Tf = 6T.\nTf = (3/2)T."},
{"id":"neet-2016-q144","pn":144,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 1 May","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Thermal expansion","q":"Coefficient of linear expansion of brass and steel rods are α₁ and α₂. Lengths of brass and steel rods are l₁ and l₂ respectively. If (l₂ - l₁) is maintained same at all temperatures, which one of the following relations holds good ?","o":["α₁l₂² = α₂l₁²","α₁²l₂ = α₂²l₁","α₁l₁ = α₂l₂","α₁l₂ = α₂l₁"],"c":2,"s":"Rise in length of brass = l₁α₁ΔT and of steel = l₂α₂ΔT.\nThe difference (l₂ - l₁) stays the same only if both rods expand by the same amount.\nSo l₁α₁ΔT = l₂α₂ΔT, giving α₁l₁ = α₂l₂."},
{"id":"neet-2016-q156","pn":156,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 1 May","tp":"Thermal Properties of Matter","st":"Wien's displacement law","q":"A black body is at a temperature of 5760 K. The energy of radiation emitted by the body at wavelength 250 nm is U₁, at wavelength 500 nm is U₂ and that at 1000 nm is U₃. Wien's constant, b = 2.88 × 10⁶ nm K. Which of the following is correct ?","o":["U₃ = 0","U₁ > U₂","U₂ > U₁","U₁ = 0"],"c":2,"s":"Wien's law: λ_m T = b.\nλ_m = 2.88 × 10⁶/5760 = 500 nm.\nThe emitted energy is greatest at 500 nm, so U₂ is larger than both U₁ and U₃.\nSo U₂ > U₁."}
